  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1258 PM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nA dry and warming pattern will persist through Friday as high \npressure builds over the region. Expect daytime temperatures to \nwarm into the upper 70s to low 80s across low elevations Friday.\n\nAn upper-level trough will work eastward with the accompanying cold \nfront bringing breezy to windy conditions on Friday. The strongest \nwinds will be across the central mountains, where gusts will range \n30 to 40 mph, while gusts will largely remain below 30 mph \nelsewhere. Precipitation will also remain further north outside of \nour CWA. Temperatures will run much cooler behind the front, with \nhighs returning to the 60s and low 70s during the weekend.\n\nThe next Pacific trough will move onshore late Sunday, bringing \nslight chances for showers and thunderstorms to Southeast Idaho \nthrough at least Monday. Expect a 5 to 10 degree cooldown between \nSunday and Monday, sending daytime highs down into the 50s and 60s. \nEnsembles suggest weak high pressure building in from the west \nbeginning Tuesday with another wave to our north suppressing the \nridge later Tuesday into Wednesday. That said, the National Blend of \nModels maintains very slight chances of precipitation across \nSoutheast Idaho for the first half of next week.",
